00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.140
This is another experiment for us, so if you want us to do more let us know in the comments.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.280
The hallmark of a thought-provoking movie is its ability to introduce a theme

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.200
and develop it through conflict, characters,
visuals, etc.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.540
This theme in theory should inspire thought or feeling for the viewer.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.360
For instance, Order vs. Chaos in the Dark Knight,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.520
Family vs. "The Family" in The Godfather,

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.300
Truth vs. Media Narrative in Gone Girl,

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.220
and the merits of punching Nazis in Captain America.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.440
In Suicide Squad, the primary theme is control.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.240
1. Dickbag guards control and abuse the main characters who are appropriately powerless prisoners.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:08.460
2. Amanda Waller and Rick Flag control the Squad with a device that kills them if they don't stay in line.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.700
3. The Enchantress battles for
control over the body of archaeologist June Moon.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.700
4. Amanda Waller controls The Enchantress by stabbing her heart when she doesn't stay in line.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.780
5. Amanda Waller also controls Rick Flag by exploiting his love affair with June.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:34.220
6. The Enchantress controls people by turning them into slave monsters.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.380
7. Enchantress's brother takes control of this guy.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.980
8. Diablo loses control and accidentally
kills his family.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.880
9. The Joker controls Harley Quinn through their abusive relationship.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.160
Her jacket even says "Property of The Joker."

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.440
We've seen this theme of control
throughout the Snyder DC Universe.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.960
In Man of Steel, we're forced to ask ourselves when metahumans with godlike powers walk the earth,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.320
how can mankind maintain control of
their own society?

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.000
The answer: Better hope one of them is going to be humanity's savior and preserve law and order.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:19.960
Then in Batman V Superman, we ask: But what if this benevolent savior goes dark and trigger-happy like Batman?

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:25.520
He has the power to wipe out the entire human race, and if we believe there's even a 1% chance that he is our enemy,

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.980
then we have to take it as an absolute certainty.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.160
How will mankind maintain order then?

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:36.640
The answer: won't happen, because Superman is motherf*cking Jesus, and he'll even sacrifice himself for humanity.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.220
This is where Suicide Squad
enters the picture:

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:45.180
Now that Earth's metahuman bodyguard is dead, how far must humanity go to maintain control?

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:58.700
If Batman V Superman explored the ramifications of a god-like being who can wield overwhelming power over humanity,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.160
then Suicide Squad is the opposite.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.860
It explores the ramifications of humans wielding overwhelming power in order to fight godlike beings.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.420
Or at least it should.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:21.780
Suicide Squad further explores this dynamic, best reflected in the painting in Batman V Superman.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.580
Lex Luthor uses it to remind us that even Satan started as an angel.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.740
He, of course, is talking about Superman,
who, like Satan, could go apeshit evil at any moment.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:44.640
Similarly, Amanda Waller is compared to the divine twice in the film, once as God and once as the Devil.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:55.360
Will Amanda Waller, a righteous government official fall like Satan in the same way Lex assumes Superman would?

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.440
Will her quest for control go too far?

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.480
So what we've got here is a pretty cool thematic setup.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.440
One that's right for all kinds of juicy moral conundrums,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.080
like is tyrannical control necessary to combat existential threats like in The Dark Knight?

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:24.780
Or is that kind of oppression evil, no matter its justification, like in One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest?

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.540
Well, Suicide Squad is neither, so a quick
recap.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:35.080
At the end, the Squad confronts Enchantress who invites them to join her and fight those who have caged them i.e.,

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.420
Amanda Waller and the government.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:46.020
The Squad says, "Screw you," kills her, and then goes back to being jailed in their Guantanamo-inspired hell.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:53.060
Herein lies the film's identity crisis: As an anti-superhero movie the film is asking you to identify with the prisoners.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:59.500
They have daughters, regrets, or are the victims of emotional abuse, and like most people are seeking freedom.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.020
So we see the humanity of the so-called
villains, while we see the lack of humanity of those in power.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:17.220
It makes sense that the anti-superhero movie would use tyrannical control

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.860
to complicate the divide between the good establishments and the evil supervillain

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.780
Even The Enchantress is a victim of control.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:33.940
A great opportunity for some thematic interplay:

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:38.860
Should we identify with The Enchantress
as we do the Suicide Squad, and if not, why?

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:45.480
Their inhumane exertion of control makes us identify with the Squad and question it's overuse by the government.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:55.640
Unfortunately, instead of developing this moral gray area, the film takes refuge in the lamest of superhero movie tropes:

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.520
Making Enchantress another bland, evil villain

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.760
who wants to destroy the world with an arbitrary doomsday machine and a super vague motivation.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:10.060
What is she talking about?

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.760
Is she lamenting that we're all glued to our Google phones and Kindle pads?

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.760
I don't see any indication of that in any DC movie, so this is just nonsense, but I digress.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.520
The film is set up to ask some really interesting questions about the characterization of evil in comic book movies.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.660
What does evil look like when our protagonists are bad guys who have been oppressed,

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:31.840
the bad guys are bad guys who have been oppressed,

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.860
and the people pulling everyone's strings are also bad guys?

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.020
What is real evil in a world like
this?

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.340
The film dances around something insightful,

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:53.420
but in the end all nuance is thrown
out the window

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:57.500
and evil is just lazily labeled as the
thing that wants to destroy the world.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.500
Why is this a problem?

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.700
Well, it robs the themes of control and
oppression of any meaning.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.960
It neither develops the statement that oppressive power is necessary,

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.380
since ultimately the Squad kills enchantress of their own accord,

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:23.340
nor does it suggest that oppressive power is evil, since Waller essentially wins and her corrupt status quo is reinstated.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:29.000
In fact, those who have been put under the control of other people almost
unilaterally get the shaft.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.960
The Enchantress dies, Diablo dies, the Squad goes back to the slammer, and Harley Quinn isn't even free from the Joker,

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.700
who treats her like a possession.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.780
So is the film saying that control is a necessary evil or does it stress the
value of freedom?

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:48.180
Neither, really.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:52.440
It's more of a movie about subjugated people defending their own imprisonment against

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:57.240
someone who's also been a victim of
control, but not in a cool way.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.740
It's just a mess.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:05.160
And speaking of messes, one of the biggest criticisms leveled at the film is that Jared Leto's Joker serves no purpose.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.600
This is mostly true; however, the only part in which the Joker

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:11.880
seems to contribute to the thematic discussion is in this line:

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.940
Okay, so desire becomes surrender, surrender becomes power.

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:31.180
Alright, does this manifest itself in the film, or is this just deliberately cryptic edge porn bullshit?

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.320
If we look at the end of the film, we sort of see this play out.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:40.340
In order to stop the Squad from thwarting her plan of world domination, The Enchantress seduces them

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:45.220
by showing them their desires: Harley dreams of a normal life with the Joker,

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:48.840
Deadshot dreams about killing Batman, and Diablo dreamsof having his family back.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:54.300
Desire makes them want to surrender, and by surrendering they would grant

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.540
Enchantress power over them, so this sort
of works, but does it actually say anything

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.400
about the nature of control and
its relation to desire and surrender?

00:10:02.409 --> 00:10:07.269
In the final cut of the film — no, but there
are hints of an attempt to do so.

00:10:07.269 --> 00:10:14.520
It seems like the writer was trying to establish an arc with Harley Quinn's relation to desire, surrender, and power.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:29.960
At the beginning, she submits to desire by letting the Joker dominate her, but then at the end she rejects desire

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.620
and doesn't let the Enchantress dominate her.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:36.140
Problem is, this isn't any kind of lesson for Harley.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:42.640
She doesn't have a paradigm shifting moment where she refuses to let her desires become the power of others.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.340
Instead, she's right back under the thumb of the Joker.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:49.380
So does this line develop the theme of control in any meaningful way?

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:55.960
So in conclusion, there's a spark of
intelligence in Suicide Squad.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.740
Enough to tell that there were likely earlier versions of the film

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.880
abandoned by studio that would have been so much better,

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:07.420
but until someone emails me a much guarded copy of the original script (wink, wink),

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.260
we can only ruminate on what may have been.

